 and pulling vertices

00:00:19.315 --> 00:00:22.745
to be able to form a model,
whatever it might be.

00:00:22.745 --> 00:00:25.065
- Such a modeler, of course.

00:00:25.065 --> 00:00:27.674
- Artist.
- Yes.

00:00:27.674 --> 00:00:29.732
What do you think of?
- I think of it as just a point

00:00:29.732 --> 00:00:31.360
out in space somewhere.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:32.860
- Yes.
- Just some location.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:34.360
- A location, yes.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.973
So, yeah, this video is called
vertex positions,

00:00:36.973 --> 00:00:40.465
and that's the way 
I think of it as a math geek.

00:00:40.465 --> 00:00:42.640
You just called me a math geek,
didn't you, before?

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.152
Yes, I love math.

00:00:44.152 --> 00:00:45.929
I even brought some math
with me today.

00:00:45.929 --> 00:00:48.909
I can't stay far, far away
from this stuff.

00:00:48.909 --> 00:00:50.839
A vertex,
it's the position

00:00:50.839 --> 00:00:52.720
of a corner.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.059
Now, these models,
we have lots of models,

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:56.560
thanks to Cassidy here.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.800
Oh, we even have this...
- Muffin.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.300
- Wow. This muffin.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.600
This muffin model moving around.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.659
So these models
are made of

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:06.822
triangles or quads or...

00:01:06.822 --> 00:01:08.825
- We work in quads,
but effect effectively,

00:01:08.825 --> 00:01:10.745
it's always triangles.

00:01:10.745 --> 00:01:12.825
Any any quad is two triangles.

00:01:12.825 --> 00:01:14.795
So, in the end,
everything's a triangle.

00:01:14.795 --> 00:01:16.457
- Right.

00:01:16.457 --> 00:01:19.152
When you think of a triangle,
I think of

00:01:19.152 --> 00:01:20.985
three corners.

00:01:20.985 --> 00:01:23.658
So, yeah, here's a vertex
position right here.

00:01:23.658 --> 00:01:25.920
Every position has an X.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.275
So the X is
the left and right

00:01:28.275 --> 00:01:30.240
of the position.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.382
It has a Y,
which is

00:01:32.382 --> 00:01:34.146
the up and down.

00:01:34.146 --> 00:01:35.885
- Depends which
software you're in.

00:01:35.885 --> 00:01:37.640
So in Unreal Engine here,

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.463
it is Z that's
up and down,

00:01:39.463 --> 00:01:41.485
and Y is forward and back.

00:01:41.485 --> 00:01:43.163
Y points forward

00:01:43.163 --> 00:01:44.918
in Unreal Engine.

00:01:44.918 --> 00:01:47.325
Something like 3ds Max,
Y points backwards.

00:01:47.325 --> 00:01:50.171
In Maya, Y points up.
Pick and choose.

00:01:50.171 --> 00:01:52.620
- I thought we talked
about that last time.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.180
"Zee" is coming up,
but the...

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.308
His computer screen
is facing

00:01:57.308 --> 00:01:59.074
flat on the table.

00:01:59.074 --> 00:02:01.676
- Well, in 2D, then
you don't have a Z at all.

00:02:01.676 --> 00:02:03.926
- Sure you do.
It comes this way.

00:02:03.926 --> 00:02:06.239
For you.

00:02:06.239 --> 00:02:08.322
- Well, anyways,
when I do this,

00:02:08.322 --> 00:02:10.454
the muffin goes up.

00:02:10.454 --> 00:02:13.135
So for math geeks like me
 and Scott, we usually think of

00:02:13.135 --> 00:02:15.405
Y as up, but you're
totally right.

00:02:15.405 --> 00:02:16.905
It it's up to the...

00:02:16.905 --> 00:02:19.002
- And it's somewhat irrelevant
because you can convert

00:02:19.002 --> 00:02:20.578
from one to the other anyways.

00:02:20.578 --> 00:02:22.125
- And that's what these
videos are about,

00:02:22.125 --> 00:02:24.454
converting between spaces.
I love that you brought that up.

00:02:24.454 --> 00:02:26.570
So that's the Y.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:29.090
The Z is the in and out.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.225
And yes.

00:02:31.225 --> 00:02:34.739
And we usually use the
right-hand rule for this.

00:02:34.739 --> 00:02:37.048
So, yeah, X is across,
 Y is up,

00:02:37.048 --> 00:02:38.905
though, yeah,
we disagree on that.

00:02:38.905 --> 00:02:40.675
And Z

00:02:40.675 --> 00:02:42.285
goes towards you.

00:02:42.285 --> 00:02:44.490
That looks right.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.780
Now, I've sneaked in another

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:49.530
coordinate here, the W.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.610
So it goes X, Y, Z, W.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:53.466
We ran out of letters,

00:02:53.466 --> 00:02:56.090
so we had to kind of
loop back around.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:59.210
Now, for vertex positions,
what is the W?

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:02.771
If I just move
the X, Y, Z over,

00:03:02.771 --> 00:03:04.777
as a programmer,
as a mathematician,

00:03:04.777 --> 00:03:06.665
the W is a choice.

00:03:06.665 --> 00:03:09.161
You either choose it to be zero,

00:03:09.161 --> 00:03:11.285
or you choose it to be one.

00:03:11.285 --> 00:03:13.617
I'm lying to you.
There's more to it than that.

00:03:13.617 --> 00:03:15.417
But just stick with me
for a second.

00:03:15.417 --> 00:03:17.463
So the idea is if W,

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:19.329
that last component of this...

00:03:19.329 --> 00:03:21.323
This is called a vector,

00:03:21.323 --> 00:03:23.160
those four components.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.673
When it's a zero,

00:03:24.673 --> 00:03:26.627
translations are not allowed.

00:03:26.627 --> 00:03:28.506
The idea of
 that is usually,

00:03:28.506 --> 00:03:31.245
you think of W as zero
for a direction.

00:03:31.245 --> 00:03:34.024
So if I'm pointing to my right,

00:03:34.024 --> 00:03:35.751
and I move around,

00:03:35.751 --> 00:03:38.285
my arm is still
pointing right.

00:03:38.285 --> 00:03:39.805
That's kind of the idea.

00:03:39.805 --> 00:03:41.766
You don't want
your direction

00:03:41.766 --> 00:03:43.341
to change as you translate.

00:03:43.341 --> 00:03:45.040
These are vertex positions.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.200
We want to be able to
translate them,

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.200
move them around.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.011
And in order to do that,

00:03:51.011 --> 00:03:53.495
the W has to be a one.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.878
So I'm going to reorganize

00:03:58.878 --> 00:04:00.885
this vector again.

00:04:00.885 --> 00:04:02.820
- Now, having four numbers,
 does that not mean

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.765
that we're in
four-dimensional space?

00:04:05.765 --> 00:04:07.685
- You had to complicate things,
didn't you?

00:04:07.685 --> 00:04:09.525
- Yeah, I need to understand.

00:04:09.525 --> 00:04:11.025
- You need to understand.

00:04:11.025 --> 00:04:13.140
Yeah, that's why I like
to take it slowly.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.871
First, I like to
think of it as a flag.

00:04:14.871 --> 00:04:17.180
True or false?
Do you want to translate or not?

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:18.820
Zero, one.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.863
But when you get deep,

00:04:20.863 --> 00:04:23.860
like Scott is being here,
are we in four dimensions?

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.132
One, two, three, four.

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:28.005
(crickets chirping)

00:04:28.005 --> 00:04:30.049
Let's talk about this.
- Okay.

00:04:30.049 --> 00:04:31.560
- Rotations, right?

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.345
Have a look at this muffin.

00:04:33.345 --> 00:04:34.945
It rotates around.

00:04:34.945 --> 00:04:36.640
Look at it go.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.401
And you can think of a rotation

00:04:39.401 --> 00:04:42.385
as rotating about a line.

00:04:42.385 --> 00:04:45.373
Now, in our video talking
about my favourite thing,

00:04:45.373 --> 00:04:47.627
quaternions, a quaternion

00:04:47.627 --> 00:04:49.810
rotates things
by making a line,

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.363
a line through the origin.

00:04:51.363 --> 00:04:52.943
And, Scott, you already
know how to

00:04:52.943 --> 00:04:54.456
do this, don't you?

00:04:54.456 --> 00:04:57.165
You set up these two planes
going through the origin.

00:04:57.165 --> 00:04:59.737
One plane's going up,
one plane's cutting it across,

00:04:59.737 --> 00:05:01.645
and you see where they meet.

00:05:01.645 --> 00:05:03.885
It meets at a line.

00:05:03.885 --> 00:05:05.805
So this is the quaternion.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.744
I'm thinking about it, right.
Okay, yes.

00:05:07.744 --> 00:05:09.877
This rotates about Z,

00:05:09.877 --> 00:05:11.430
about this line.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.350
So that's two planes.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.790
The idea is
the big reveal.

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:19.193
- See if I can get three.
- Don't be nervous here.

00:05:19.193 --> 00:05:21.290
What if there are
three planes?

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:23.418
What if you cut three planes

00:05:23.418 --> 00:05:25.111
into each other?

00:05:25.111 --> 00:05:27.245
Well, let's see.
So you got that one, that one.

00:05:27.245 --> 00:05:29.025
- You're going
to have to hold it.

00:05:29.025 --> 00:05:30.971
- I'll hold it.
There you go.

00:05:30.971 --> 00:05:33.170
So that's going to go on there.
- Hey, perfect.

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:34.700
- There you go.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.228
- Now have a look
where it meets.

00:05:36.228 --> 00:05:38.080
Meets right in this corner,

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:39.933
and that is a point.

00:05:39.933 --> 00:05:41.709
If these three planes,

00:05:41.709 --> 00:05:43.821
they are at 90 degrees
to each other,

00:05:43.821 --> 00:05:45.341
they're going to the origin,

00:05:45.341 --> 00:05:47.969
that point there is W.

00:05:47.969 --> 00:05:49.533
So kind of the idea...

00:05:49.533 --> 00:05:52.174
- And just define a position

00:05:52.174 --> 00:05:54.229
as just X, Y, Z
somewhere in space.

00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:55.815
- Well, you could.

00:05:55.815 --> 00:05:57.595
- To be the meet
of three things.

00:05:57.595 --> 00:06:00.375
- Yes, so that's
kind of one way

00:06:00.375 --> 00:06:02.955
to think about this W,

00:06:02.955 --> 00:06:04.584
this one.

00:06:04.584 --> 00:06:07.120
It's almost like
start at the origin.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:08.960
Start at the origin, then move

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.800
X, Y, Z across.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.676
But if W is zero,

00:06:12.676 --> 00:06:15.760
it's like that point
is infinitely far away.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.256
The idea is, 
if I swap it round,

00:06:19.256 --> 00:06:22.510
this would be a normal,
this would be a direction.

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:24.983
This point is infinitely
far away, and it's just like

00:06:24.983 --> 00:06:26.636
pointing at a star.

00:06:26.636 --> 00:06:28.405
If you point at it and
you move around,

00:06:28.405 --> 00:06:30.387
you're still pointing at the
star because it's so far away.

00:06:30.387 --> 00:06:33.389
So these are
the four coordinates

00:06:33.389 --> 00:06:35.240
typically used.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:36.999
But like I'm saying,
 programmers just set

00:06:36.999 --> 00:06:39.444
the W to one,
and then they're happy

00:06:39.444 --> 00:06:42.280
to do the next
transformations they need.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:44.087
Now, these vertices,

00:06:44.087 --> 00:06:45.640
I think they were
made by you, right?

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.505
They're made in model space?

00:06:47.505 --> 00:06:50.479
- Yeah, they're produced
in a bunch of different ways.

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:52.564
But, yeah, essentially,
we're making models,

00:06:52.564 --> 00:06:54.784
whether it be procedural,
drag out a sphere,

00:06:54.784 --> 00:06:57.116
a teapot that
we talked about,

00:06:57.116 --> 00:06:59.394
or actually starting with

00:06:59.394 --> 00:07:01.663
individual planes
and modeling

00:07:01.663 --> 00:07:03.554
something more complicated
with polygons, yeah.

00:07:03.554 --> 00:07:06.037
- So looking at this
muffin over here,

00:07:06.037 --> 00:07:09.255
could you, like, guess
how many vertices on this?

00:07:09.255 --> 00:07:11.330
- I don't know what
the guess would be.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.087
There could be
anywhere from 20

00:07:13.087 --> 00:07:14.956
to, you know, 10,000,

00:07:14.956 --> 00:07:16.520
but it looks like
probably closer to

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.061
20 or something.
I don't know.

00:07:18.061 --> 00:07:19.653
- 20 vertices?

00:07:19.653 --> 00:07:21.168
- Maybe only, yeah.

00:07:21.168 --> 00:07:22.700
I mean, it could be 40.
I don't know.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:25.479
- And this muffin is in
Cassidy's coffee shop.

00:07:25.479 --> 00:07:27.561
So this is world space,

00:07:27.561 --> 00:07:29.398
where this muffin is right now,

00:07:29.398 --> 00:07:31.297
but these vertices are
made by the artist

00:07:31.297 --> 00:07:32.810
in model space.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:34.998
So there's three operations

00:07:34.998 --> 00:07:36.845
we've got to do
to go from

00:07:36.845 --> 00:07:39.690
model space to world space.

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:41.653
First, we've got to be able

00:07:41.653 --> 00:07:43.232
to rotate.

00:07:43.232 --> 00:07:44.927
We do want to be able
to rotate

00:07:44.927 --> 00:07:46.825
the muffin.

00:07:46.825 --> 00:07:49.639
We also want to be able
to translate the muffin.

00:07:49.639 --> 00:07:51.385
And the last one is,
say you're really hungry,

00:07:51.385 --> 00:07:52.985
you want a massive muffin.

00:07:52.985 --> 00:07:55.145
So there's also the scale.

00:07:55.145 --> 00:07:57.260
There's the scale,
rotate, translate.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:07:59.779
And typically, what you do,
you multiply them

00:07:59.779 --> 00:08:02.163
in the correct order,
and that gives you

00:08:02.163 --> 00:08:04.050
the model matrix.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:06.389
- Yeah.
Hey, that's my world.

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:07.970
- Yeah.

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:10.497
- So I'll take
 your vertices,

00:08:10.497 --> 00:08:12.930
multiply them
by my model matrix.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.390
And then I can move
the muffin around

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:18.535
in the world space,
as you're calling it.

00:08:18.535 --> 00:08:20.651
And then I'll multiply
 by a few more matrices

00:08:20.651 --> 00:08:22.352
that I'm sure you'll
talk about in a moment.

00:08:22.352 --> 00:08:24.455
- Usually, I just think of
the model matrix.

00:08:24.455 --> 00:08:27.402
That just sets up the model
into world space,

00:08:27.402 --> 00:08:29.667
but there's other things
you can do with vertices,

00:08:29.667 --> 00:08:31.487
other ways to mess them up.

00:08:31.487 --> 00:08:34.630
I wanted to ask you about
shaders in particular.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:37.655
I've heard of this thing
called a vertex shader.

00:08:37.655 --> 00:08:39.682
- Oh, dear.
So on the graphic card,

00:08:39.682 --> 00:08:42.390
we have a fictitious thing.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.870
They call it a pipeline,
but it's not really a pipeline.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:46.508
It's just some, it's code

00:08:46.508 --> 00:08:48.845
on the on the graphics card.

00:08:48.845 --> 00:08:51.027
And what it's going to do is

00:08:51.027 --> 00:08:53.536
for every vertex you send me,

00:08:53.536 --> 00:08:55.905
I'm going to run a little
tiny program on it

00:08:55.905 --> 00:08:59.130
that will move it
into the world space

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:02.830
and all the other spaces
that we're talking about.

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:04.988
I'm going to move each one
of those vertex,

00:09:04.988 --> 00:09:06.718
and I'm going to
call this little program

00:09:06.718 --> 00:09:08.510
for every single vertex.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:10.241
So you say there's 40 here,

00:09:10.241 --> 00:09:13.085
but there's probably 40,000
in the entire cafe

00:09:13.085 --> 00:09:15.065
we have to generate.

00:09:15.065 --> 00:09:17.361
And so I'm going to run
this little tiny program

00:09:17.361 --> 00:09:19.568
on a graphics card
in what's called

00:09:19.568 --> 00:09:21.113
the vertex shader,
and I'm going to run it

00:09:21.113 --> 00:09:22.628
40,000 times.

00:09:22.628 --> 00:09:25.457
And then it goes down
this fictitious pipeline.

00:09:25.457 --> 00:09:27.341
And then every...

00:09:27.341 --> 00:09:29.288
Since we're always
drawing in triangles,

00:09:29.288 --> 00:09:32.407
every three times I call
the vertex shader,

00:09:32.407 --> 00:09:34.668
that's going to
 create a primitive.

00:09:34.668 --> 00:09:37.920
The primitive that we usually 
use is that triangle.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:40.505
And then that goes
down the pipeline

00:09:40.505 --> 00:09:42.345
where it'll get rasterized.

00:09:42.345 --> 00:09:44.602
So now I'm going to
find out what pixels are

00:09:44.602 --> 00:09:47.945
surrounding that triangle,

00:09:47.945 --> 00:09:50.345
what pixels are involved
in that triangle.

00:09:50.345 --> 00:09:52.665
And then I'm going
to run a second program.

00:09:52.665 --> 00:09:55.000
The second program
is the fragment shader,

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:57.691
and I'm going to run
that for every single pixel.

00:09:57.691 --> 00:10:00.280
That program, I'm going to run
it over and over and over

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:02.984
for every pixel in the scene.

00:10:02.984 --> 00:10:04.597
Crazy, right?

00:10:04.597 --> 00:10:06.995
So all this information 
has to come down the pipeline.

00:10:06.995 --> 00:10:09.273
But the original
manipulation occurs

00:10:09.273 --> 00:10:11.270
just in the vertex shader.

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:13.452
- And are there other kind of

00:10:13.452 --> 00:10:15.845
shader-like programs?

00:10:15.845 --> 00:10:18.178
- Yeah, so this fictitious
pipeline we're talking about,

00:10:18.178 --> 00:10:20.135
there are little entry points
that I can get into

00:10:20.135 --> 00:10:22.060
where I can mess with things.

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:24.300
So one of them is
a geometry shader.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.329
So once I run the program
three times

00:10:27.329 --> 00:10:29.286
and I've generated the triangle,

00:10:29.286 --> 00:10:31.826
down the tube it goes,
down the down the pipeline,

00:10:31.826 --> 00:10:33.467
and then I can
intercept it

00:10:33.467 --> 00:10:35.535
at the geometry stage.

00:10:35.535 --> 00:10:38.095
And in the geometry stage,
I'm looking now

00:10:38.095 --> 00:10:40.484
at the primitive itself,
 and I can twist

00:10:40.484 --> 00:10:43.177
the primitive scale
or change it, move it.

00:10:43.177 --> 00:10:45.666
And then just before you get
to the geometry shader,

00:10:45.666 --> 00:10:48.107
there's another set of little

00:10:48.107 --> 00:10:50.169
doorways I can get into,
and that's called

00:10:50.169 --> 00:10:51.750
the tessellation shaders.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.250
- Oh, that's a long word.
Tessellation...

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:54.750
- Tessellation shaders, yeah.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:57.190
What I'm going to do there
is if you give me one triangle,

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:59.176
I can take that one triangle
 and cut it up into

00:10:59.176 --> 00:11:01.768
10,000 little triangles,
all right,

00:11:01.768 --> 00:11:04.745
and manipulate those guys
a little bit, change them down.

00:11:04.745 --> 00:11:06.878
Then they will go
into the geometry shader,

00:11:06.878 --> 00:11:09.108
and then each one of those
 10,000 little ones,

00:11:09.108 --> 00:11:11.305
I could individually mess with.

00:11:11.305 --> 00:11:12.927
And then each one
of those then goes

00:11:12.927 --> 00:11:14.513
into the fragment shader,

00:11:14.513 --> 00:11:16.864
which is where I'm going to
colour them all in.

00:11:16.864 --> 00:11:19.326
I'm a busy guy.
- Yeah, no kidding.

00:11:19.326 --> 00:11:21.252
- I make your stuff look good.

00:11:21.252 --> 00:11:22.829
- Yeah, no.
Without question.

00:11:22.829 --> 00:11:25.694
So we use that tessellation
for displacements.

00:11:25.694 --> 00:11:29.078
So when you want to get really,
really fine detail rendered out

00:11:29.078 --> 00:11:31.381
instead of having it
all modeled, it actually...

00:11:31.381 --> 00:11:34.531
the textures will tell

00:11:34.531 --> 00:11:36.622
your system that you need to

00:11:36.622 --> 00:11:39.008
fragment this triangle
down really small

00:11:39.008 --> 00:11:41.259
and then start displacing
those little vertices

00:11:41.259 --> 00:11:42.838
so that it looks
like there's...

00:11:42.838 --> 00:11:45.189
- My guys thought
 that we would say you model

00:11:45.189 --> 00:11:46.977
like a head and...

00:11:46.977 --> 00:11:49.820
but I want to put
the skin pores on.

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:51.909
And so I'll do that with
tessellation so I get in there

00:11:51.909 --> 00:11:54.298
and finely retune that.

00:11:54.298 --> 00:11:56.420
That'd be a crazy
amount of verts.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:58.233
- Wow.
And these are shaders

00:11:58.233 --> 00:11:59.740
that you're talking about?

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:01.804
- It's kind of a weird
word that they use

00:12:01.804 --> 00:12:03.947
because they say each one
of these stages is a shader,

00:12:03.947 --> 00:12:05.457
then the entire thing
is a shader.

00:12:05.457 --> 00:12:06.985
- Oh, wow.

00:12:06.985 --> 00:12:08.577
- And so I don't like
calling it that.

00:12:08.577 --> 00:12:10.225
I like calling these things
little sub shaders.

00:12:10.225 --> 00:12:11.725
They build the shader.

00:12:11.725 --> 00:12:14.417
And then in other languages,
they call it just the pipeline.

00:12:14.417 --> 00:12:16.710
The whole thing is a pipeline.

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:18.630
And that's actually probably
a more accurate word.

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:21.293
It's a pipeline, but it really
is just a mathematical construct

00:12:21.293 --> 00:12:24.349
inside the graphic art.

00:12:24.349 --> 00:12:25.850
- And as I was...

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:27.852
As you were talking
about things there, Paul,

00:12:27.852 --> 00:12:30.233
I was noticing
that your bending arms.

00:12:30.233 --> 00:12:32.914
